# Semgrep findings that are accepted on purpose, with the reason.
#
# 1. The Semgrep community ruleset expects the pnpm 10 supply-chain settings
#    (minimumReleaseAge / trustPolicy / blockExoticSubDependencies) in the pnpm
#    workspace file. pnpm 12 ships its own supply-chain policy and verifies the
#    lockfile on every install ("Verifying lockfile against supply-chain
#    policies"), it no longer reads those settings from package.json, and the
#    current stack (Expo SDK 58, Better Auth 1.7.10) was published inside the
#    last week, so a seven-day window would block the whole project. The
#    versions are pinned exactly, the audits in scripts/check-audit.mjs fail on
#    any high or critical advisory, and the licence inventory covers the
#    licensing risk. Tracked as the W3 exception in CONSTRAINTS.md.
#
# 2. The container rule wants a read-only root filesystem. The stateful
#    services here write to their data directory (Postgres, PostGIS, Valkey),
#    which is a named volume, not the container filesystem; a read-only root
#    would stop them from starting. The no-new-privileges rule is honoured
#    everywhere.

package_managers.pnpm.pnpm-missing-minimum-release-age
package_managers.pnpm.pnpm-trust-policy
package_managers.pnpm.pnpm-block-exotic-sub-dependencies
package_managers.npm.npm-missing-minimum-release-age
yaml.docker-compose.security.writable-filesystem-service.writable-filesystem-service

# 3. The same rule for uv: the API pins its versions in uv.lock and the
#    security patches (certifi) have to land the day they are published, so a
#    seven-day cooldown would keep the vulnerable version in place. uv 12
#    verifies the lockfile against its own supply-chain policy on every
#    install, the versions are pinned exactly, the audits fail on any high or
#    critical advisory, and the licence inventory covers the licensing risk.
package_managers.uv.uv-missing-dependency-cooldown